# notes of slides 35-68 of the chapter 5 deck 3.8.0
N = {}
N[35] = """Child number 0 lies in the normal range, below 2 to the power 31. Child number 2147483648 is the first hardened child and gives a different key from child 0, so the comparison prints False. In a path, the letter h adds 2 to the power 31 to the index, which the example confirms: the path m/0h has the single index 2147483648, which is the constant HARD, and the comparison prints True. The table lists the three cases. The letter h in a path is therefore a security decision, because hardened children cannot be derived from a public key. This is code 13 of 27 on the course page."""
N[36] = """A normal child private key, together with the parent's extended public key, exposes the parent private key. The example takes a parent key and its public key K, derives the normal child number 5, and then calls a function that works backwards: the extended public key plus one normal child private key, minus the hash value, gives the parent private key. The run prints True, which means the parent private key was recovered. A hardened step cannot be undone this way, because its hash is computed from the private key. This is why hardening protects the parent. The four boxes show the subtraction, and this is code 14 of 27."""
N[37] = """Public keys can be derived without private keys. The example derives child number 5 in two ways. Route one derives the private child and then computes its public key. Route two uses only the parent public key K and the chain code. Both routes end at the same public key, and the comparison prints True. This property lets a web shop make a fresh address for every customer while holding no private key at all, which slide 58 shows. It works only for normal children, because a hardened child needs the private parent key. This is code 15 of 27 on the course page."""
N[38] = """An extended key is a key together with its chain code, written in Base58Check. The example serialises the master private key as an xprv and the master public key as an xpub; both are 111 characters long and begin with xprv and xpub. The fingerprint of the master key is 3442193e, four bytes that identify it, and the depth of the master key is 0 because it sits at the top of the tree. An xprv can spend and derive, while an xpub can only watch and derive public keys. This is code 16 of 27, and the table lists the four values that the run printed."""
N[39] = """The book writes 2,147,483,647 as the number of children of each kind, while BIP32 counts 2 to the power 31 for each kind. The example prints 2147483648, the value of HARD, and 2147483647, the last normal index. It then counts the two ranges: the normal indexes from 0 to 2^31 minus 1 and the hardened indexes from 2^31 to 2^32 minus 1 each hold 2147483648 children. The two kinds are equal in number. The book's figure is the last index, not the count. This is code 17 of 27, and it is one of the places where the book differs from the standard."""
N[40] = """This table lists the concepts of the tree in part 4 and the slides that show them. The master private key is the first private key of the tree, and the master chain code is the 32 bytes that let children be made. Child key derivation makes a new key from a parent key, a chain code and an index. The chain code is 32 bytes that keep derivation unpredictable, and the index number runs from 0 to 2^31 minus 1 for normal children, then hardened. Together they describe how one master pair produces every key of the wallet, with a numbered index choosing the child."""
N[41] = """This table lists the extended keys of part 4. An extended key is a key plus its chain code in one string. The extended private key, xprv, can spend and derive. The extended public key, xpub, derives public keys only, so it can show every address of a wallet but cannot move funds. Hardened derivation is a step that needs the private parent key, which keeps a leaked xpub from exposing the private keys above it. The rule that follows is to give out the xpub when a program needs to watch or make addresses, and never to give out the xprv."""
N[42] = """The third table of part 4 lists the sections of the book. The section on hierarchical deterministic key generation, BIP32, describes one seed and a tree of keys. Creating an HD wallet from the seed gives the master key and chain code. Private child key derivation makes a child private key from the parent private key, and using derived child keys explains why hardened children never expose the parent. Extended keys are a key plus a chain code as xprv or xpub. Public child key derivation derives public keys from a public key, hardened child derivation needs the private parent key, and the last row covers index numbers: 0 to 2^31 minus 1 normal, 2^31 and up hardened."""
N[43] = """Part 5 asks which key is which and how to write it down. Six concepts are defined: the HD wallet path, the numbers from the master key down to one key; BIP43 and BIP44, in which the first number names the scheme and BIP44 fixes five levels; implicit paths, which a standard fixes and which therefore need not be recorded; explicit paths, written down next to the recovery code; output script descriptors, text that names the script and the keys behind it; and the key origin, the bracket that names a key's parent fingerprint and path. The diagram runs from the recovery code through a path and a descriptor to the addresses."""
N[44] = """A path is a list of child numbers below the master key m. The example reads the path m/84h/0h/0h/1/7 into its numbers and labels them with the five levels that BIP43 and BIP44 define: purpose, coin, account, change and index. Printing each number together with whether it is hardened shows that the first three are hardened and the last two are normal, so the count of hardened levels is 3. The diagram marks the hardened levels in orange: purpose 84h, coin 0h and account 0h. Change is 1 and index is 7. This is code 18 of 27 on the course page."""
N[45] = """Four standard paths give four address types from one test recovery code. Purpose 44 gives a legacy pay-to-public-key-hash address, which starts with 1. Purpose 49 wraps a segwit script inside a script hash, which starts with 3. Purposes 84 and 86 give native segwit, which starts with bc1q, and taproot, which starts with bc1p. The table lists the first address of each, for example bc1qcr8te4kr609gcawutmrza0j4xv80jy8z306fyu for purpose 84. Each address follows from the same words and a different purpose number, which is why a backup must record which paths were used. This is code 19 of 27."""
N[46] = """The example derives the first address of the BIP84 test recovery code at the path m/84h/0h/0h/0/0 and compares it with the address that BIP84 publishes and that Bitcoin Core derives, bc1qcr8te4kr609gcawutmrza0j4xv80jy8z306fyu. The comparison prints True. The table shows the two sources side by side with the same address and an Equal row that reads True. Checking a library against a published test vector is how a reader knows that the derivation is correct before trusting it with real keys. The path here is written explicitly, which is the point of the slide. This is code 20 of 27."""
N[47] = """A key tweak turns a public key into a related key. The example derives the internal key of the BIP86 path and then computes the taproot output key by adding a number derived from a hash, multiplied by the generator point. The first 12 hexadecimal digits of the internal key are cc8a4bc64d89 and those of the output key are a60869f0dbcf. The taproot address encodes the tweaked key, not the internal one, and it begins bc1p5cyxnuxmeuwuvkwfem96lqzszd02n6xd. Adding a number to a key gives a related key, and that is the whole idea of the tweak. This is code 21 of 27."""
N[48] = """Paths are either implicit or explicit. An implicit path is fixed by a standard such as BIP44, so the wallet does not record it. An explicit path is written down with the recovery code, usually in descriptor notation. The table lists the four purposes as the book gives them: 44 for BIP44 with the path m/44'/0'/0' for legacy scripts, 49 for BIP49 with m/49'/1'/0' where coin type 1 is testnet in SLIP-0044, 84 for BIP84 with m/84'/0'/0' for native segwit, and 86 for BIP86 with m/86'/0'/0' for single-key taproot. Know which paths are implicit and write down the rest."""
N[49] = """An output script descriptor is one line of text that names the script and the keys behind it. The example prints the last eight characters of the checksum of the descriptor that Bitcoin Core made, afwvtk2s, which BIP380 appends after the hash sign. It then parses the key origin in the bracket at the start, which gives the parent fingerprint 73c5da0a and a hardened path: all three steps are True. Finally it builds a two-of-three multisignature descriptor, wsh(multi(2,A,B,C)), and its own checksum ends uhe03042. One line of text can rebuild the structure of a wallet. This is code 22 of 27."""
N[50] = """Descriptors grow in three ways. A multisignature descriptor lists several keys and requires signatures from at least a threshold of them: in the diagram keys A, B and C sit inside wsh(multi(2,A,B,C)), and any two may sign. A multipath descriptor holds a tuple in one path step, such as the receiving and the change branch, in a single descriptor. Miniscript is a language for a subset of scripts that supports analysis and generic signing. In each case the descriptor keeps the rules of the wallet in one line of text that can be saved with the backup."""
N[51] = """This table lists the concepts of part 5 with the slides that show them. The HD wallet path is a list of numbers from the master key to one key. BIP43 sets the purpose number below the master key, and BIP44 fixes five levels: purpose, coin, account, change and index. Implicit paths are fixed by a standard and not recorded, whereas explicit paths are written down with the backup. Output script descriptors are text that names the script and the keys. A path names exactly one key, and a descriptor names the whole structure of a wallet, so a backup needs one or the other."""
N[52] = """The second table of part 5 lists the sections of the book. The section on backing up key derivation paths says that paths or descriptors must be saved with the recovery code. The HD wallet key identifier is the path, and m/84h/0h/0h/0/0 names exactly one key. Navigating the HD wallet tree structure means following the levels purpose, coin, account, change and index. If the path is not written down with the key, a restored wallet may derive from the wrong path and show an empty balance, even though every word of the code is right."""
N[53] = """Part 6 shows that a recovery code is not the whole backup. Six concepts are defined: a label, a note that names an address or a transaction; BIP329, a standard file format for exporting labels; the Lightning Network, which makes payments off the blockchain with their own state; static channel backups, a one-time backup that helps recover channel funds; encryption, which turns readable data into data that only a key holder reads; and the encrypted wallet backup, the whole database encrypted by a key from the seed. The diagram runs from the keys from the seed to labels, protocol data and an encrypted backup."""
N[54] = """A label is a note that names an address or a transaction, and it is not derived from the seed. BIP329 writes one JSON object per line, so any program can read the backup. The example builds two rows, one address labelled rent and one transaction labelled pay, and prints each as a JSON line; the table shows the type, the first characters of the reference and the label. Two rows are exported, which the result line confirms. Labels are data too, and a restored wallet that shows the right balance but no descriptions has lost them. This is code 23 of 27 on the course page."""
N[55] = """Two answers exist for data that the keys cannot rebuild. A static channel backup is saved once when a channel opens; if data is lost, the backup helps close the channel, but it cannot guarantee a result. An encrypted wallet backup holds the whole wallet database, with keys, labels and protocol data, and is made often and automatically. It is encrypted with a key derived from the seed, stored as a copy, and decrypted by the seed at restore time. The rule is that keys come back from the seed and everything else needs its own copy."""
N[56] = """On 21 March 2014, three weeks after the bankruptcy filing, Mark Karpeles, chief executive of Mt. Gox, said the exchange had found about 200,000 BTC. Simon Sharwood of The Register reported it. The coins sat in an old-format wallet file that the company believed was empty and had never checked. A file made by software two generations older still held spendable keys. Wallet files hold keys for as long as they exist, so a file should be labelled and tested and never discarded blindly. The picture is the Mt. Gox logo, in the public domain, and the link goes to the report."""
N[57] = """Part 7 puts keys to work and tests the backup. Six concepts are defined: extended public key deployment, an xpub placed where it makes addresses; the payment processor, software that turns an xpub into a working shop; the gap limit, how many unused addresses in a row a scan checks; cold storage, keys kept where no connected computer can read them; the written backup, the recovery code on a physical medium; and testing a backup, a restore drill before it is needed. The diagram shows the order: the xpub on the shop, a customer paying, keys staying cold and a restore drill."""
N[58] = """A web shop can accept payments without holding any private key. The example parses the book's extended public key, decompresses its key, and derives public children 0, 1 and 2 with the chain code. For each customer it prints a fresh address: bc1qv32edz2zpvhmmjkd7vq2wcnhacys0pku9vh5jw, bc1qtqvqy3kyl5y6dmvk65p7e966k8ycyhwg9ypw3p and bc1qw399x6vyt7jhurm7h3ylc0uldlqr7wj9clk5ng. No private key exists on the server, so a break-in cannot steal the funds. Publish the xpub, and the keys never touch the internet. This is code 24 of 27 on the course page."""
N[59] = """The gap limit decides how far a scan looks. In the example a wallet received payments on keys 0, 3 and 25. The scan stops after a number of unused keys in a row, and the example tries gaps of 5, 10, 20 and 30. Gaps below 22 miss key 25, because there are 21 unused keys between key 3 and key 25, so only the gap of 30 reaches it and finds all three keys. A scan that stops too early misses money that is still in the wallet. This is why a restore may need a larger gap. This is code 25 of 27."""
N[60] = """A digital signature binds one key to one message. The example fixes a toy private key and its public key, hashes the message pay 1 and signs the hash. The signature verifies for that message and prints True. It fails for any other message, such as pay 2, and prints False. The nonce in the example is fixed to keep the run repeatable; a real wallet must never choose it by hand. This is the property that lets a hardware signing device sign a transaction without revealing its key: anyone can verify, and nobody without the key can sign. This is code 26 of 27."""
N[61] = """Cold storage means keys created and stored offline, which the book advises for any significant holding. The diagram shows the flow: a wallet on a connected computer holds only public keys and prepares an unsigned transaction, the offline signing device signs it, and the signed transaction returns for broadcast. A hardware signing device signs transactions and never shows the private keys. The written backup is the recovery code on paper or metal, stored apart from the device. Together they give offline keys, a device that signs and a code on paper, and a thief would need all three places."""
N[62] = """The restore drill tests whether a backup works. The example defines a helper that derives the first address of a recovery code at the path m/84h/0h/0h/0/0, and the address of the BIP84 test code is written down as noted when the wallet is created. Restoring from the right code gives the same address, so the first comparison prints True. Changing one word, about to above, gives a different wallet, and the second comparison prints False. A single wrong word therefore opens another, empty wallet with no error. Test the restore, not your luck. This is code 27 of 27 on the course page."""
N[63] = """A complete backup holds five things: the recovery code, the passphrase if one is used, the paths or descriptors, the public keys of other signers in a multisignature wallet, and the labels and protocol data. After the five items comes the test: restore from the backup alone and check the first address. The closing remark says that data loss is nobody's fault but cannot be reversed, so the holder makes the backups and tests them. A backup is complete only when a restore drill succeeds, because only a restore shows that nothing is missing."""
N[64] = """The last table lists the concepts of parts 6 and 7 with the slides that show them. A label is text naming an address or a transaction, and BIP329 stores one JSON line per label. A static channel backup helps recover channel funds but gives no guarantee. The gap limit is how many unused addresses in a row a scan checks, and the section called Mind the Gap explains it. Backing up nonkey data keeps labels and protocol data apart from the seed, and an extended public key on a web store lets the shop make a fresh address for each customer without any private key."""
N[65] = """Six questions test the chapter, and the table names the code slide that answers each one. Why is one seed better than many loose keys? Code 1. How many words does 256 bits of entropy make? Code 5. What does the checksum protect against? Code 7. Why does a passphrase change the wallet? Code 10. Why are account levels hardened? Code 14. How do you know a backup works? Code 27. A reader should answer each question without looking and then check the answer against the code slide and its printed result."""
N[66] = """The chapter in five lines: wallets stopped storing keys and started deriving them, so one seed is backed up once. A recovery code is entropy in words, eleven bits a word with the checksum included. Words become a 512-bit seed by deliberate slowness, and a passphrase forks the wallet. The HD tree gives every purpose a numbered key, and hardened steps protect the parent. Beyond keys come paths, labels, channels and a backup that counts only once it has been restored. Each line summarises one group of slides, and the code slides of that group carry the proof."""
N[67] = """The resources are grouped by kind, and every entry is a live link. Reading: Mastering Bitcoin chapter 5, the New York Times story through Business Standard and the Register report on the 200,000 coins. Standards: BIP 39 for mnemonic codes, BIP 32 for HD wallets, BIP 84 for P2WPKH derivation and BIP 380 for output script descriptors. The seminal work is the Bitcoin paper. The tools are Ian Coleman's BIP39 tool, to be used offline, and BTCPay Server. A tutorial, two community forums, the course repository and a video on BIP-39 words complete the list. The course page holds the same code."""
N[68] = """After this chapter a reader should be able to say four things, each with its proof in the deck. Why one seed replaced bags of keys: code 1 shows that one seed gives every key again. What twelve words carry and how they become a tree: codes 6 to 12 show eleven-bit words, the stretch to 512 bits and the master key. Which path a wallet walks and who else can walk it: codes 18 to 22. What a tested backup is: code 27, where one wrong word opens another wallet. The slide closes the deck and invites questions."""
